## Objective ("Find")
The nutcracker mechanism is modeled as a lever system designed to crack a macadamia nut. The required output force at the nut is 2000 N. The input force, originally 
assumed to be applied by hand, is 300 N and is now replaced by a linear actuator. The distance from the pivot to the nut is 25 mm, and the distance from the pivot to 
the end of the handle is 170 mm, which was determined from the required mechanical advantage. The total handle length is therefore 170 mm. In the updated model, the 
handles are no longer assumed to be rigid and are instead treated as beams that deform under transverse loading. The design must ensure that the maximum vertical deflection
of the handle remains below 2% of its total length.

## Constraints ("Given")
- The nutcracker must generate at least 2000N at the nut to successfully crack it
- The maximum vertical deflection of the handle must be less than 2% of its total length
- The handle must be designed to be mass-efficient by maximizing stiffness while minimizing material usage
- Only transverse components of the applied forces are considered in the beam analysis
- The original geometry of the nutcracker, including lever arm distances, must remain unchanged

